        "text": "{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"reasons\":[\"The rule's preferred position expressly requires Customer's indemnity to be limited to Customer-provided content and Customer's breach of law, so the overbroad limb in Section 8.2(a)(3) (indemnifying IBM for any Services Recipient claim 'related, directly or indirectly, to the Services', and excluded from our cap under 9.2(a)) is a genuine material deviation and edges into the walk-away concept of a Customer indemnity for Vendor's own services.\",\"The finding correctly declines to redline IBM's IP indemnity in Section 8.1, which sits at or above the fallback (usual exclusions, procure/modify/replace/credit remedies, carved out of IBM's cap), and only notes the absent preferred limbs (breach of law, data breach, wilful misconduct) in the rationale rather than flagging them — consistent with the classification semantics and avoiding over-flagging.\",\"The redline is a targeted, single-limb substitution ('that arises out of Customer's breach of applicable law') that reaches the playbook position while leaving the Content/AUP and Customer Components limbs intact; the high changed-character ratio is inherent to replacing one enumerated limb and is acceptable here.\",\"The failed 'vendor indemnity for IP infringement present' regex is a phrasing artifact — p0089 contains an express IBM defend/indemnify/hold harmless obligation for patent, trademark, copyright and trade secret claims — so it does not undermine the finding.\",\"The comment is accurate, in the required first-person-plural tone, states what changed, cites the playbook position on customer indemnity scope, and confirms we accept IBM's IP indemnity as drafted.\"]}"
